  second.id = "alcaraz_poetry_legs_tracked_second"; second.role = "second"; second.note = "Same body motion as the first serve: Ryan's clip has one serve and he asked for both to follow it. The game flies the ball as a second serve (kick). First serve recaptured from Ryan's poetry-in-motion clip (one serve, ad side, platform stance, right-handed). Court axes: x player's right, y up, z into the court. The toss hand is in front and to his right, which fixes those signs. Hip travel on the ground is measured from the baseline and the service line. Across the jump, depth is interpolated and the height comes from how far the heel sits above that line (estimated). Each joint keeps the identity it had on the first frame. A detection that leaves its parent, or that swaps left with right, is dropped and filled from the frames around it. The racket arm from the toss through the landing is one path: up to the ear, high through the drop, then up and into the court at contact. The measured walk-up is kept. The trophy is a platform stance: feet apart, each knee on its own side, front knee over the toes. The legs do not cross. At contact the body is off the ground and the chest leans into the court. The racket arm is reattached afterwards in the same shape, so the legs cannot move it. Real-time scale is estimated: toss release to contact = 0.90 s. Overall scale 1.156 anchors thigh+shank to 0.48 of stature (estimated proportion). No other player's serve is changed. Legs and arms re-fitted (alcaraz_legs_arms.py): each knee follows the overlay's angle frame by frame (its 3D estimate for the kicked-back right leg after landing); planted feet stay put and the hips rise or sink to fit; a foot in the air turns about its knee. Arms follow the clip's 2D track through one fitted camera (rms 54 px); each bone keeps its length and the part hidden by the camera angle (folding toward or away from the lens) comes from that length and the 3D capture's side. Hands are matched to the 3D capture so they never swap where they cross, and hands and knees never overlap.";
